      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© American Academy of Otolaryngology\u2014Head and Neck Surgery Foundation 2015.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To evaluate the impact of early versus delayed endoscopic sinus surgery (ESS) in terms of postoperative health care utilization, using a patient cohort with chronic rhinosinusitis (CRS)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
            "content": "Retrospective administrative database analysis."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SETTING",
            "content": "US-based primary and secondary sites of care."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SUBJECTS AND METHODS",
            "content": "CRS patients with ESS in 2010-with no other ESS before 2010 and with complete medical history from 2004 to 2012-were identified within the MarketScan database. Patients were characterized by time interval of first sinusitis or nasal polyposis diagnosis to ESS and grouped as following: group 1, <1 year (n=818); group 2, 1 to <2 years (n=247); group 3, 2 to <3 years (n=274); group 4, 3 to <4 years (n=364); group 5, 4 to <5 years (n=595); and group 6, ≥5 years (n=535). Outpatient visits/procedures and prescriptions associated with sinusitis and/or nasal polyps were analyzed for 1 year preoperatively and 2 years postoperatively. Subanalyses were conducted on separate cohorts with or without asthma or polyps, within each group."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Patients in group 1 had significantly fewer visits and prescriptions than patients in group 6 (postoperative visits: group 1, 4.45 [95% CI, 4.06-4.84]; group 6, 6.70 [95% CI, 6.10-7.30; prescriptions: group 1, 4.54 [95% CI, 4.12-4.96]; group 6, 7.61 [95% CI, 6.92-8.31]). Gradual increases in utilization were observed from groups 1 to 6. Subanalysis of patients with and without asthma or polyps showed similar findings."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Early intervention after diagnoses of CRS, with or without asthma or polyps, is associated with lower health care utilization than intervention after many years of medical management."
          }
        ]
      },
